🔥 Regular Expression Matching
🧩 Problem Statement

Given two strings:

s → input string

p → pattern string

Implement regex matching with support for:

Symbol	Meaning
.	Matches any single character
*	Matches zero or more of the previous element

Return true if the entire string matches the pattern.

📌 Important Rules

Pattern must match entire string

* always refers to previous character

Only these two special characters exist: . and *

🧪 Example 1
Input:
s = "aa"
p = "a"

Output: false


Reason: Pattern only matches one 'a'

🧪 Example 2
Input:
s = "aa"
p = "a*"

Output: true


Reason: a* → zero or more 'a'

🧪 Example 3 (Very Important)
s = "aab"
p = "c*a*b"
Output = true


Why?

c* → zero 'c'

a* → matches "aa"

b → matches "b"

❌ Why Greedy or Recursion Alone Fails?

Because:

* creates multiple choices

Overlapping subproblems

Exponential branching

👉 So we use Dynamic Programming

🧠 Core Idea

Let:

dp[i][j]


means:

Does first i characters of s
match first j characters of p?

🏗 DP Table Size
(m+1) x (n+1)


Because we include empty string cases.

🪜 Step-by-Step Algorithm
STEP 1️⃣ Initialization
dp[0][0] = true


Empty string matches empty pattern.

STEP 2️⃣ Handle Patterns Like "a*" or "ab"

If pattern contains *, it may match empty string.

Example:

p = "a*b*"


So:

if p[j-1] == '*'
    dp[0][j] = dp[0][j-2]


Why j-2?
Because a* can match zero characters.

STEP 3️⃣ Filling the Table

Now iterate:

for i in 1 to m
  for j in 1 to n

CASE 1: Normal Character or '.'

If:

p[j-1] == s[i-1]
OR
p[j-1] == '.'


Then:

dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1]


Why?
If last characters match, reduce problem size.

CASE 2: When Pattern Has '*'

If:

p[j-1] == '*'


Two possibilities:

Option 1️⃣ Zero Occurrence

Ignore the x*

dp[i][j] = dp[i][j-2]

Option 2️⃣ One or More Occurrence

If previous char matches:

p[j-2] == s[i-1]
OR
p[j-2] == '.'


Then:

dp[i][j] |= dp[i-1][j]


Why?
Stay in pattern, reduce string.

🔎 Full Dry Run (Important)
Input
s = "aab"
p = "c*a*b"


Lengths:
m = 3
n = 5

DP table → 4 × 6

Initialize
dp[0][0] = true


Now handle empty string vs pattern:

c* → can be zero
a* → can be zero


So first row becomes:

T F T F T F


Now fill table step by step:

When reaching:

s = a a b
p = c * a * b

Eventually:

dp[3][5] = true

Because:

c* → zero

a* → match aa

b → match b

Final answer = true

💻 Full Java Code
public class RegexMatching {

    public boolean isMatch(String s, String p) {
        int m = s.length();
        int n = p.length();
        
        boolean[][] dp = new boolean[m + 1][n + 1];
        
        dp[0][0] = true;

        // Handle empty string vs pattern
        for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
            if (p.charAt(j - 1) == '*') {
                dp[0][j] = dp[0][j - 2];
            }
        }

        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {

                char sc = s.charAt(i - 1);
                char pc = p.charAt(j - 1);

                // Case 1: exact match or '.'
                if (pc == sc || pc == '.') {
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1];
                }

                // Case 2: '*'
                else if (pc == '*') {

                    // Zero occurrence
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i][j - 2];

                    char prev = p.charAt(j - 2);

                    // One or more occurrence
                    if (prev == sc || prev == '.') {
                        dp[i][j] |= dp[i - 1][j];
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return dp[m][n];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        RegexMatching sol = new RegexMatching();
        System.out.println(
            sol.isMatch("aab", "c*a*b")
        );
    }
}

⏱ Complexity Analysis
Type	Complexity
Time	O(m × n)
Space	O(m × n)
